; A bare identifier in pattern position that names a constructor.
;
; `recordCoverage` reads a `TAG_E_VAR` pattern as a catch-all binder,
; so this arm binds the scrutinee to a variable called `None` and takes
; the first arm for every value. Nothing objected: it is well-typed, it
; covers no constructor so exhaustiveness is satisfied by the
; catch-all, and there is no unreachable-arm detection to notice that
; the later arms are dead.
;
; Measured before 2026-08-17:
;
;   (:: f (-> (Option Int) Int))
;   (fn (f o) (match o (None 111) ((Some x) x)))
;   (f (Some 42))     ->  111, at exit 0
;
; That is a silent wrong answer, the category MM-VAL-9b names as the
; worst outcome, and it is what every ML-family language in a reader's
; background spells this way. The built-in `Option` guarantees a new
; user meets it in their first hour.
;
; The old behaviour was deliberate - stage0's parser makes every bare
; identifier a variable pattern - and `tests/selfhost/485-bare-ctor-
; binder.ax` pinned it, with an expectation of 5 for a value that is a
; `Cons2`. A fixture that pins a wrong answer is the argument for
; changing the rule rather than against it.
;
; The fix is machine-applicable and safe to auto-apply: `None` and
; `(None)` are the same span rewritten into a form the pattern grammar
; accepts, which is the property AX3012's fix did not have (see 190).
; A lowercase binder is unaffected - the test is whether the spelling
; RESOLVES to a constructor, not what case it is written in.

(data Shape (Circle Int) (Square Int))

; Refused: `Circle` here binds, it does not test.
(:: area (-> Shape Int))
(fn (area s)
  (match s
    (Circle 1)
    ((Square w) (* w w))))

; Accepted, and unchanged: an ordinary binder that names no
; constructor is still a catch-all.
(:: firstOr (-> Shape Int))
(fn (firstOr s)
  (match s
    ((Circle r) r)
    (other 0)))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) (firstOr (Circle 3)))
